.TH 9SHARE.CONF 5 "2026" "9share" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
9share.conf \- what a machine shares and what it shows, for 9share
.SH DESCRIPTION
The configuration of
.BR 9share (8)
is written in obconf, the notation of the OpenBSD daemons' configuration files: keywords and their arguments, braces for a
block, words that may be put in double quotes, and
.B #
for a comment that runs to the end of the line.  There are two files, with the same format.  An
.B export
block belongs in the user's
.I ~/.config/9share/9share.conf
(or
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/9share/9share.conf ),
a
.B mount
block in
.IR /etc/9share/9share.conf .
.PP
.B 9share export
and
.B 9share mount
write the blocks (and
.B unexport
and
.B unmount
take them away) without changing anything else in the file, so comments and the layout the author gave it stay.
The files may be edited by hand; the daemon notices within a few seconds.
.SS export
.nf
export "projects" {
        directory /srv/projects
        host bob.example.org
}
.fi
.TP
.I name
(after the word
.BR export )
Letters, digits and . _ \- (not starting with . _ \-), at most 64.  The receiving machine uses the same name.
.TP
.B directory
The directory to share: an absolute path of letters, digits and . _ + @ \- / (no spaces).  Required.
.TP
.B host
Where to send it, as
.BR ssh (1)
knows it: a name from ~/.ssh/config, or user@host.  ssh must work from this machine to it without a password.  Required.
.SS mount
.nf
mount "projects" {
        user alice
        at /mnt/projects
}
.fi
.TP
.I name
The name the exporter gave the share.
.TP
.B user
The account on this machine that the exporting machine logs in as.  The share arrives in that account's
.IR ~/.9share/ .
Required.
.TP
.B at
The mount point: an absolute path of letters, digits and . _ + @ \- / (no spaces), created if it is missing.  Required.
.PP
Both blocks take one more setting:
.TP
.B hold
How many seconds a connection waits for a new carrier (a new ssh connection) after it was lost, before the session is given up (and the
mount removed): 1 to 86400, default 600.  While it waits the mount stays and what the kernel asks waits too.  The exporter and the
receiver each use their own: give both the same.
.PP
A name may be used once in a file, and a mount point once.  A block with a mistake in it is reported as
.IR file:line:\ message
and left out; the other blocks are used.  A mount point that already holds something that is not 9p is left alone.
.PP
The package installs
.I /etc/9share/9share.conf
as a template of comments with no blocks: nothing is shared and nothing is mounted until a block is added.
.B 9share setup
writes the usual blocks after a few questions.
.SH FILES
The secret that makes a share's link prove itself with SCRAM-SHA-256 is not in the configuration: it is the file
.I ~/.config/9share/NAME.secret
on each machine, made by
.BR "9share pair" .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR 9share (8),
.BR 9share\-setup (1)
